    <item>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://mastodon.gamedev.place/@djlink/117212080306513283</guid>
      <link>https://mastodon.gamedev.place/@djlink/117212080306513283</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 04 Sep 2026 09:33:28 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;It’s hard to explain but it’s a mix from I would not recommend someone to get it in terms of future proof, but also yes it’s the perfect device for a lot of people to play a ton games; I don’t regret getting one but also it’s so expensive and I think about it often. Maybe in several months if PC parts become cheaper I might feel more remorse, if parts become more expensive might pass. I don’t think it’s about the device itself but the idea of the specs/price in a small pocket of timing.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://mastodon.gamedev.place/@djlink/117212049206690853</guid>
      <link>https://mastodon.gamedev.place/@djlink/117212049206690853</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 04 Sep 2026 09:25:34 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;In terms of performance, the most intensive I tested was CONTROL and some of the recent Resident Evil games. At 1080p and even 1440p I had no issues with high settings at 60fps, they perform well. But it’s just so easy to tweak resolution “let see how this does” that you see the bottleneck right away, it’s the GPU, not RAM. For most past forget about any Raytracing, it works but you probably have to go for lower settings/lower res which is not ideal. FSR does wonders in some games.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
